-- Q1: medium / L3 — Section 304 brother-sister corp stock acquisition
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Whitaker owns 100% of both Alpha Corp and Beta Corp. Whitaker sells all of his Alpha Corp stock (basis $80,000) to Beta Corp for $250,000. Alpha Corp has $60,000 of E&P and Beta Corp has $100,000 of E&P. Under Section 304, how is the $250,000 payment treated?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$160,000 dividend (combined E&P) and $10,000 capital gain", "$100,000 dividend (Beta E&P only) and $150,000 capital gain", "$250,000 capital gain because it is a sale of stock", "$60,000 dividend (Alpha E&P only) and $190,000 return of capital"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 304(a)(1), a sale of stock of one controlled corporation to another is treated as a redemption. Because Whitaker owns 100% before and after (applying Section 318), no Section 302(b) test is met, so the entire amount is treated as a Section 301 distribution. Combined E&P of $160,000 ($60,000 + $100,000) is taxed as a dividend. The remaining $90,000 reduces basis ($80,000 return of capital) leaving $10,000 capital gain. Wrong (B): This incorrectly limits the dividend to Beta Corp's E&P alone. Section 304(b)(2) requires using the combined E&P of both corporations. Wrong (C): This ignores Section 304 entirely. The transaction is not treated as a simple stock sale when the corporations are brother-sister related. Wrong (D): This incorrectly limits the dividend to Alpha Corp's E&P and misapplies the excess as return of capital without recognizing gain.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q2: medium / L3 — S corp AAA ordering with accumulated E&P
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Brennan is the sole shareholder of Vega Corp, an S corporation that was previously a C corporation. At year-end, Vega has an accumulated adjustments account (AAA) of $40,000, accumulated E&P of $70,000 from its C corp years, and makes a $90,000 cash distribution to Brennan. Brennan's stock basis is $120,000. How is the distribution taxed?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$90,000 tax-free return of capital reducing stock basis", "$40,000 tax-free, $50,000 dividend, and $0 capital gain", "$90,000 dividend from accumulated E&P", "$70,000 dividend and $20,000 tax-free return of basis"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 1368(c), S corporation distributions are applied first against AAA ($40,000 tax-free, reducing stock basis to $80,000), then against accumulated E&P ($50,000 taxed as dividend), and any remainder reduces stock basis. Here the full $90,000 is allocated: $40,000 from AAA (tax-free) and $50,000 from accumulated E&P (dividend). Wrong (A): This ignores both the AAA ordering and the accumulated E&P layer. With accumulated E&P present, distributions cannot bypass the dividend layer entirely. Wrong (C): This incorrectly treats the entire distribution as coming from accumulated E&P, ignoring the mandatory AAA-first ordering. Wrong (D): This reverses the ordering by applying accumulated E&P before AAA, which contradicts Section 1368(c).$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q3: medium / L3 — C corp distribution ordering (E&P → basis → capital gain)
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Langford owns all shares of Crescent Corp (C corporation) with a stock basis of $30,000. Crescent has current E&P of $20,000 and accumulated E&P of $15,000. Crescent distributes $60,000 cash to Langford. How is the distribution taxed to Langford?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$60,000 ordinary dividend", "$35,000 dividend, $25,000 return of capital, and $0 capital gain", "$35,000 dividend, $25,000 capital gain", "$35,000 dividend, $0 return of capital, and $25,000 capital gain"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 301(c), distributions are taxed first as dividends to the extent of E&P ($20,000 current + $15,000 accumulated = $35,000), then as a tax-free return of capital reducing stock basis ($25,000 remaining reduces basis from $30,000 to $5,000). Since the remaining $25,000 does not exceed basis, there is no capital gain. Wrong (A): This incorrectly treats the entire $60,000 as a dividend, ignoring the E&P limitation. Distributions exceed E&P by $25,000. Wrong (B): This correctly identifies the dividend and return of capital but fails to recognize that the remaining $25,000 does not produce capital gain since it is less than basis. Wrong (D): This treats the excess over E&P as immediate capital gain, ignoring the return-of-capital step that first reduces stock basis.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q4: medium / L2 — Partnership Section 751 hot assets in distribution
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Martinez is a 25% partner in Oak Partnership. Oak distributes $100,000 cash to Martinez in a liquidating distribution. Martinez's outside basis is $60,000. Oak's only assets are $200,000 cash and $200,000 of unrealized receivables (accounts receivable with zero basis). What is the character of Martinez's $40,000 gain?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$40,000 long-term capital gain", "$40,000 short-term capital gain", "$20,000 ordinary income and $20,000 long-term capital gain", "$40,000 ordinary income"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 751(b), unrealized receivables are "hot assets." When a partner receives a disproportionate share of non-hot assets (cash) relative to hot assets (receivables), the gain attributable to the partner's share of hot assets is ordinary income. Martinez's 25% share of receivables ($50,000) is exchanged for cash, producing ordinary income on that portion. The entire $40,000 gain is ordinary. Wrong (A): This incorrectly characterizes all gain as capital gain, ignoring the Section 751 hot asset rules. Wrong (B): Short-term capital gain is not the correct character; Section 751 produces ordinary income, not capital gain of any holding period. Wrong (C): This incorrectly splits the gain. Because the receivables have zero basis, all gain attributable to Martinez's exchanged share of hot assets is ordinary.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q5: easy / L1 — Definition of foregone interest under Section 7872
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$What is "foregone interest" as defined under Section 7872 for below-market loans?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The difference between interest at the applicable federal rate and the actual interest charged", "The total interest that would have been charged at the prime rate", "Any interest not collected by the lender after the loan matures", "The penalty interest assessed by the IRS on delinquent tax payments"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 7872(e)(2), foregone interest is the excess of the amount of interest that would have been payable at the applicable federal rate (AFR) over the interest actually payable on the loan. This difference triggers imputed income and deduction consequences. Wrong (B): The prime rate is a commercial benchmark, not the statutory rate used under Section 7872. The AFR, published monthly by the IRS, is the required minimum rate. Wrong (C): Uncollected interest after maturity is a collection issue, not the definition of foregone interest under the below-market loan rules. Wrong (D): Penalty interest on delinquent taxes falls under Section 6601, not the below-market loan provisions of Section 7872.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — Section 302(c)(2) family attribution waiver conditions
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Pemberton owns 40% of Cascade Corp and her father owns 60%. Cascade redeems all of Pemberton's shares for $200,000 (basis $75,000). Pemberton wants to waive family attribution under Section 302(c)(2) so the redemption qualifies as a complete termination under Section 302(b)(3). Which set of conditions must Pemberton satisfy to waive family attribution?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Hold no interest in the corporation for 5 years after the redemption and file a waiver with the IRS", "File a 10-year IRS notification agreement, retain no interest (not as officer, director, or employee) except as creditor, and have no related-party stock acquisitions in the prior 10 years", "Transfer all redeemed shares to an unrelated third party within 12 months of the redemption date", "Obtain written consent from all remaining shareholders and file a Section 302(c)(2) election within 90 days"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): IRC Section 302(c)(2) permits waiver of family attribution for complete termination redemptions if three conditions are met: (1) the redeemed shareholder retains no interest in the corporation (including as officer, director, or employee) except as a creditor, (2) the shareholder files an agreement to notify the IRS of any stock reacquisition within the 10-year period, and (3) the shareholder has not acquired stock from a related person within the prior 10 years to avoid tax. Wrong (A): The relevant period is 10 years, not 5 years, and this omits the written agreement and no-interest requirements. Wrong (C): There is no requirement to transfer redeemed shares to a third party. The corporation acquires and retires the shares in a redemption. Wrong (D): Shareholder consent is not required. The waiver is between the redeemed shareholder and the IRS, not dependent on other shareholders' approval.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: hard / L4 — Multi-entity Section 304 analysis with E&P
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$A CPA advises Harrington, who owns 100% of both Apex Corp (E&P: $200,000) and Summit Corp (E&P: $50,000). Harrington's basis in Apex stock is $120,000. Harrington proposes selling all Apex stock to Summit for $350,000. The CPA must evaluate the tax consequences under Section 304 and recommend whether Harrington should instead sell the Apex stock to an unrelated buyer offering $340,000. Which analysis is correct?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The Section 304 sale produces $250,000 dividend and $0 capital gain; the third-party sale produces $220,000 capital gain — the third-party sale is more tax-efficient at preferential rates", "The Section 304 sale produces $350,000 capital gain; it is better than the third-party sale because of the higher sale price", "The Section 304 sale produces $250,000 dividend, $0 return of capital, and $100,000 capital gain — the third-party sale at $340,000 would produce less total tax despite lower gross proceeds", "The Section 304 sale produces $200,000 dividend and $150,000 capital gain — the third-party sale is marginally worse due to lower proceeds"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under Section 304(a)(1), the sale to Summit is treated as a redemption. Harrington owns 100% before and after, so no Section 302(b) test is met. The $350,000 is a Section 301 distribution: $250,000 dividend (combined E&P of $200,000 + $50,000), then $100,000 return of capital (reducing basis from $120,000 to $20,000), and $0 capital gain since the excess does not exceed remaining basis. The third-party sale yields $340,000 - $120,000 = $220,000 capital gain at preferential rates. Despite $10,000 less in proceeds, the all-capital-gain character may produce lower total tax than $250,000 of dividend income. Wrong (A): This miscalculates the Section 304 result by not properly accounting for the return-of-capital step and incorrectly shows $0 capital gain without explaining why. Wrong (B): This ignores Section 304 entirely, incorrectly treating the related-corporation sale as a simple stock sale producing only capital gain. Wrong (D): This uses only Apex's E&P ($200,000) rather than the combined E&P of both corporations ($250,000) as required by Section 304(b)(2).$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q8: medium / L3 — S corp basis ordering (income → distributions → losses)
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Rivera is the sole shareholder of Falcon S Corp. At the start of the year, Rivera's stock basis is $10,000. During the year, Falcon reports $50,000 of ordinary income and $30,000 of separately stated long-term capital loss. Falcon also distributes $45,000 cash to Rivera mid-year. What is Rivera's stock basis at year-end, and how much capital loss can she deduct?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Stock basis $0; deductible capital loss $15,000", "Stock basis $15,000; deductible capital loss $30,000", "Stock basis $0; deductible capital loss $30,000", "Stock basis $0; deductible capital loss $15,000 with $15,000 suspended"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 1368 and Reg. 1.1368-2, basis adjustments follow a specific order: (1) increase for income ($10,000 + $50,000 = $60,000), (2) decrease for distributions ($60,000 - $45,000 = $15,000), (3) decrease for losses (limited to remaining basis of $15,000). Rivera deducts $15,000 of the $30,000 capital loss, and the remaining $15,000 is suspended under Section 1366(d)(1). Stock basis is $0. Wrong (A): This states the correct basis but fails to identify that $15,000 of loss is suspended, implying the full loss was deducted or permanently disallowed. Wrong (B): This incorrectly applies losses before distributions, leaving $30,000 fully deductible and $15,000 remaining basis after the distribution. The ordering rules require distributions before losses. Wrong (C): This incorrectly allows the full $30,000 loss deduction. Only $15,000 of basis remains after income and distributions, limiting the deductible loss.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q9: hard / L4 — Guaranteed payment timing across fiscal years
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$A CPA advises Blake, a calendar-year taxpayer and 50% partner in Meridian Partnership, which has a January 31 fiscal year-end. Meridian accrues a $120,000 guaranteed payment to Blake for management services on January 15, 2026 (within Meridian's fiscal year ending January 31, 2026). Blake receives the cash on March 1, 2026. The CPA must determine in which tax year Blake reports the guaranteed payment income. What is the correct analysis?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Blake reports the $120,000 in his 2026 calendar year because the partnership's fiscal year ending January 31, 2026 falls within Blake's 2026 tax year, and guaranteed payments are included when the partnership deducts them regardless of when cash is received", "Blake reports the $120,000 in his 2025 calendar year because the partnership accrued the payment in a fiscal year that began in 2025", "Blake reports the $120,000 in his 2026 calendar year because he received the cash in 2026 and partners use the cash method for guaranteed payments", "Blake reports $60,000 in 2025 and $60,000 in 2026, prorated based on the partnership fiscal year straddling both calendar years"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 706(a), a partner includes guaranteed payments in the partner's tax year within which the partnership's tax year ends. Meridian's fiscal year ends January 31, 2026, which falls within Blake's 2026 calendar year. Therefore Blake reports the full $120,000 in 2026 regardless of when cash is received. Guaranteed payments follow partnership year-end inclusion rules, not the cash method. Wrong (B): Although the fiscal year began in 2025, the operative date is when the partnership year ends (January 31, 2026), which falls in Blake's 2026 tax year. Wrong (C): While the result is correct (2026), the reasoning is wrong. The timing is not based on cash receipt but on the partnership's year-end. If Blake had received cash in 2025, the answer would still be 2026. Wrong (D): There is no proration of guaranteed payments across calendar years. The entire amount is included in the partner's year that contains the partnership year-end.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q10: hard / L4 — Liquidating distribution loss recognition
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Caldwell has an outside basis of $180,000 in JMK Partnership. In a complete liquidation of Caldwell's interest, the partnership distributes $50,000 cash, inventory with an inside basis of $40,000 (FMV $60,000), and a capital asset (equipment) with an inside basis of $30,000 (FMV $45,000). No unrealized receivables are distributed. Caldwell's CPA must determine whether Caldwell recognizes a loss and, if so, its character. What is the correct analysis?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Caldwell recognizes a $90,000 ordinary loss because the distribution total FMV ($155,000) is less than basis ($180,000)", "Caldwell recognizes a $90,000 capital loss computed as $180,000 basis minus $90,000 total inside basis of distributed assets", "Caldwell recognizes no loss because the distribution includes a capital asset (equipment), which prevents loss recognition; the excess basis ($130,000) is allocated to the distributed property under Section 732(b)", "Caldwell recognizes a $25,000 loss based on the difference between basis and total FMV of distributed assets"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 731(a)(2), a partner recognizes loss on a liquidating distribution ONLY if the distribution consists solely of money, unrealized receivables, and/or inventory. Here, Caldwell also receives equipment (a capital asset), so no loss is recognized. The remaining basis ($180,000 - $50,000 cash = $130,000) is allocated among the inventory and equipment under Section 732(b). Wrong (A): Loss recognition is barred when a capital asset is included in the distribution, regardless of FMV. Section 731(a)(2) restricts loss to distributions of only money, receivables, and inventory. Wrong (B): No loss is recognized because a capital asset was distributed. The basis comparison is irrelevant when the eligibility test for loss recognition is not met. Wrong (D): This uses FMV to compute the loss, but FMV is not the measure. Moreover, no loss is recognized at all because the distribution includes a capital asset.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q11: medium / L3 — Section 311(b)(2) liability exceeding FMV on property dividend
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Clearwater Inc., a C corporation, declares a property dividend of land (basis $60,000, FMV $150,000) encumbered by a $180,000 mortgage assumed by the recipient shareholder. The mortgage exceeds FMV by $30,000. Under Section 311(b)(2), what gain must Clearwater recognize, and why does the liability affect the computation?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$90,000 — computed as FMV ($150,000) minus basis ($60,000), ignoring the mortgage entirely", "$60,000 — the corporation only recognizes gain on the equity portion of the property", "$120,000 — when the liability exceeds FMV, Section 311(b)(2) treats FMV as equal to the liability ($180,000), producing gain of $180,000 minus $60,000 basis", "$150,000 — the full fair market value is treated as the amount realized"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 311(b)(2), when distributed property is subject to a liability exceeding its FMV, the FMV is deemed to be not less than the amount of the liability. The gain is $180,000 (deemed FMV) minus $60,000 basis = $120,000. This prevents corporations from distributing underwater mortgaged property to shift economic losses. Wrong (A): This ignores the liability floor rule. When the mortgage exceeds FMV, the corporation cannot use the lower FMV to compute gain. Wrong (B): There is no "equity portion" concept in Section 311(b). The gain computation uses the higher of FMV or liability minus basis. Wrong (D): While $150,000 is the actual FMV, Section 311(b)(2) substitutes the $180,000 liability because it exceeds FMV, making $150,000 the wrong figure.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q12: hard / L4 — Section 7872 demand vs term loan OID comparison
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$A CPA advises Kingsley, who lends $400,000 to her C corporation. She is considering either a demand loan at 0% interest or a 5-year term loan at 0% interest. The current AFR for demand loans is 5% and for 5-year term loans is 4%. The CPA must explain how the imputed interest calculations differ. Which analysis is correct?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Both loans are treated identically — $20,000 imputed interest per year ($400,000 x 5%) regardless of demand or term structure", "The demand loan imputes interest quarterly at variable rates; the term loan has no imputed interest because it has a fixed maturity date", "The demand loan has $20,000 annual imputed interest; the term loan has $16,000 annual imputed interest, both recalculated each year at the current AFR", "The demand loan imputes approximately $20,000/year but the amount changes annually as the AFR fluctuates; the term loan locks in approximately $16,000/year based on the 4% AFR at issuance, calculated under OID rules"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 7872(a), demand loans are retested each year using the current semiannual AFR (which may change), producing approximately $20,000 at today's 5% rate. Under Section 7872(b), term loans compute foregone interest at issuance using OID rules, locking in the AFR at the time the loan is made (4%), yielding approximately $16,000/year. The key difference is that demand loan rates float while term loan rates are fixed at issuance. Wrong (A): This ignores the different AFR rates for demand versus term loans and the different computational methods. The term loan uses 4%, not 5%. Wrong (B): Term loans do have imputed interest under Section 7872(b). A fixed maturity does not exempt them. The exemption applies only to loans under the de minimis thresholds. Wrong (C): This incorrectly states both are recalculated annually. Only the demand loan is recalculated at current rates. The term loan rate is locked at the AFR in effect when the loan was made.$EXPL$, 'hard', 4),

-- Q13: medium / L2 — Section 267 constructive ownership through entities
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Newman owns 30% of Pinecrest Corp directly and owns a 60% interest in Birch Partnership, which owns 40% of Pinecrest Corp. Newman sells equipment at a $25,000 loss to Pinecrest Corp. Under Section 267, Newman's constructive ownership includes his direct shares plus his proportionate share through entities. What is Newman's total ownership of Pinecrest for Section 267 purposes, and is the loss disallowed?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["54% (30% + 60% x 40%); the loss is disallowed because Newman owns more than 50%", "30%; the loss is allowed because indirect ownership through partnerships does not count under Section 267", "70% (30% + 40%); the loss is disallowed", "30% direct only; partnership attribution applies only under Section 318, not Section 267"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): Under IRC Section 267(c)(1), stock owned by a partnership is considered owned proportionately by its partners. Newman constructively owns 24% through Birch Partnership (60% x 40%) plus 30% directly = 54%. Since 54% exceeds the 50% threshold, the $25,000 loss is disallowed under Section 267(a)(1). Wrong (B): Section 267(c)(1) explicitly attributes partnership-owned stock to partners proportionately. Indirect ownership does count. Wrong (C): This adds the full 40% partnership ownership instead of Newman's proportionate share (60% x 40% = 24%). Attribution is proportional to the partner's interest. Wrong (D): Section 267(c) has its own constructive ownership rules separate from Section 318. Partnership attribution applies under both statutes.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q14: medium / L3 — Section 318 double attribution (family→family prohibition)
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Dawson owns 100% of Ridgeway Corp. Dawson's son, Tyler, owns no stock in Ridgeway. Tyler's wife, Megan, also owns no stock. A CPA is analyzing whether Megan constructively owns Ridgeway stock through family attribution under Section 318. Dawson's stock would be attributed to Tyler (father→son), and Tyler's constructive stock would then be attributed to Megan (husband→wife). Is this "double family" attribution permitted?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Yes, because Section 318 allows unlimited chain attribution through family members", "No — stock attributed to Tyler from his father cannot be re-attributed from Tyler to Megan under the family attribution rules; double family attribution is prohibited under Section 318(a)(5)(B)", "Yes, but only if Dawson, Tyler, and Megan all live in the same household", "No, because in-laws are never considered related parties under Section 318"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): IRC Section 318(a)(5)(B) prohibits "double family" attribution — stock constructively owned by an individual through family attribution cannot be re-attributed to another family member. Dawson's stock is attributed to Tyler (parent→child), but that constructive ownership cannot then be re-attributed from Tyler to Megan (spouse). Megan does not constructively own Ridgeway stock. Wrong (A): Section 318 does not allow unlimited chain attribution. The anti-double-family rule in Section 318(a)(5)(B) specifically prevents this cascading. Wrong (C): Household status is irrelevant. The prohibition on double family attribution applies regardless of living arrangements. Wrong (D): Spouses are included in Section 318 family attribution. The issue is not that in-laws are excluded, but that double family chain attribution is prohibited.$EXPL$, 'medium', 3),

-- Q15: easy / L2 — Bargain purchase from corporation as constructive dividend
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Hoffman, the sole shareholder of Sterling Corp (C corporation with $300,000 of E&P), purchases a corporate-owned vehicle from Sterling for $15,000. The vehicle's fair market value is $45,000. What is the tax consequence to Hoffman?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$15,000 capital gain on the purchase", "No tax consequence because Hoffman paid consideration for the vehicle", "$30,000 constructive dividend representing the bargain element (FMV minus purchase price)", "$45,000 constructive dividend representing the full FMV of the vehicle"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 301, when a shareholder purchases corporate property below FMV, the difference between FMV and the amount paid is a constructive dividend to the extent of E&P. Hoffman received a $30,000 benefit ($45,000 - $15,000), taxed as a dividend since Sterling has sufficient E&P. Wrong (A): This is not a capital gain transaction. The bargain element is a constructive dividend, not a sale generating gain to the buyer. Wrong (B): Payment of partial consideration does not eliminate the tax consequence. The shortfall from FMV is treated as a distribution of corporate earnings. Wrong (D): Only the bargain element ($30,000) is a constructive dividend, not the full FMV. Hoffman paid $15,000, so only the $30,000 difference represents the shareholder benefit.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q16: medium / L2 — Section 318 attribution from estate or trust to beneficiary
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$The Kershaw Family Trust owns 500 shares of Orion Corp. Beneficiary Collins has a 40% actuarial interest in the trust. Under Section 318(a)(2)(B), how many shares of Orion Corp are attributed to Collins?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["500 shares, because beneficiaries are attributed all stock owned by a trust", "None, because trust-to-beneficiary attribution only applies to grantor trusts", "250 shares, because beneficiaries are attributed half of trust-owned stock regardless of their interest", "200 shares, because beneficiaries are attributed stock proportional to their actuarial interest in the trust"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 318(a)(2)(B)(i), stock owned by a trust is considered owned by its beneficiaries in proportion to their actuarial interest in the trust. Collins's 40% actuarial interest means 200 shares (500 x 40%) are attributed to Collins. Wrong (A): Attribution is proportional to actuarial interest, not 100%. A beneficiary is attributed only their proportionate share of trust-owned stock. Wrong (B): Section 318(a)(2)(B) applies to all trusts (other than certain qualified retirement trusts), not only grantor trusts. Wrong (C): There is no 50% default rule. The attribution is based on the specific actuarial interest of each beneficiary, which here is 40%.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q17: medium / L2 — Section 1239 ownership threshold (more than 50%)
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Crawford owns exactly 50% of Vertex Corp. Crawford sells depreciable equipment to Vertex Corp for $200,000, recognizing a gain of $75,000. Under Section 1239, how is the gain characterized?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Section 1231 gain, because Section 1239 applies only when the seller owns more than 50%", "Ordinary income, recharacterized under Section 1239 for depreciable property", "Disallowed entirely under Section 267 as a related party transaction", "Blended treatment: 50% ordinary income and 50% capital gain"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): IRC Section 1239 recharacterizes gain as ordinary income only when the seller and the purchasing entity are related, defined as more than 50% ownership (directly or constructively). Crawford owns exactly 50%, which does not exceed the threshold. The gain retains its Section 1231 character. Wrong (B): Section 1239 requires ownership of MORE than 50%. At exactly 50%, the provision does not apply. This is a frequent exam distinction. Wrong (C): Section 267 disallows losses between related parties, not gains. Crawford has a gain on this sale, so Section 267(a)(1) does not apply. Wrong (D): There is no blended treatment under Section 1239. Either the entire gain is recharacterized as ordinary income or it retains its original character.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — C corp distribution with zero E&P
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$How is a cash distribution from a C corporation treated when the corporation has zero current and accumulated earnings and profits (E&P)?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The distribution is always taxable as ordinary income", "The distribution is a tax-free return of capital that reduces the shareholder's stock basis, with any excess treated as capital gain", "The distribution is a nontaxable gift from the corporation to the shareholder", "The distribution is deductible by the corporation as a business expense"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 301(c), distributions are dividends only to the extent of E&P. With zero E&P, the distribution reduces the shareholder's stock basis (tax-free return of capital). Any amount exceeding basis is treated as gain from the sale or exchange of property (capital gain). Wrong (A): Distributions are taxed as ordinary dividend income only to the extent of E&P. With no E&P, there is no dividend component. Wrong (C): Corporate distributions are not gifts. They are governed by Section 301 and have specific tax consequences even without E&P. Wrong (D): Distributions to shareholders are never deductible by the corporation. Dividends are paid from after-tax earnings.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q19: easy / L1 — Section 302(b)(2) two numerical tests
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$What two numerical requirements must a stock redemption meet to qualify as "substantially disproportionate" under Section 302(b)(2)?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The shareholder must own less than 25% after the redemption and less than 50% of total shares", "The shareholder's ownership must drop by at least 20 percentage points and the shareholder must own less than 50%", "After the redemption, the shareholder must own less than 50% of total voting power AND the shareholder's voting stock percentage must be less than 80% of the pre-redemption percentage", "The corporation must redeem at least 50% of the shareholder's shares and the shareholder must not be a family member of any other shareholder"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): IRC Section 302(b)(2) requires two tests: (1) after the redemption, the shareholder owns less than 50% of the total combined voting power, and (2) the shareholder's percentage of voting stock after the redemption is less than 80% of the percentage owned before the redemption. Both must be satisfied. Wrong (A): There is no 25% threshold in Section 302(b)(2). The tests are based on 50% voting power and the 80% ratio. Wrong (B): The test is not a fixed 20-percentage-point drop. It uses the 80% ratio, which produces different required drops depending on the starting percentage. Wrong (D): Section 302(b)(2) does not require a minimum redemption percentage or exclude family members. Family relationships are relevant to constructive ownership under Section 318 but are not a separate test.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q20: medium / L2 — Section 267(d) disallowed loss cannot create or increase a loss
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Foster sells stock with a basis of $100,000 to his daughter for $70,000. The $30,000 loss is disallowed under Section 267. The daughter later sells the stock to an unrelated buyer for $65,000. Her basis is $70,000 (purchase price). How does Section 267(d) affect the daughter's recognized loss?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The daughter recognizes a $35,000 loss ($100,000 original basis minus $65,000 sale price)", "The daughter recognizes a $5,000 loss ($70,000 basis minus $65,000); the father's disallowed loss cannot reduce the daughter's loss further", "The daughter recognizes no loss because the previously disallowed loss was transferred to her", "The daughter recognizes a $5,000 loss; the previously disallowed $30,000 loss cannot be used to create or increase a loss on the subsequent sale"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 267(d), the previously disallowed loss can only offset gain on a subsequent sale by the related buyer. It cannot create or increase a loss. The daughter's basis is $70,000, so her realized loss is $5,000 ($70,000 - $65,000). The father's disallowed $30,000 loss provides no additional benefit here because there is no gain to offset. Wrong (A): The daughter's basis is $70,000 (her purchase price), not the father's original basis of $100,000. Disallowed losses do not transfer basis. Wrong (B): While the loss amount ($5,000) is correct, the reasoning incorrectly implies the disallowed loss could theoretically reduce a loss further. Section 267(d) only offsets gain. Wrong (C): The daughter does recognize her own loss of $5,000. The previously disallowed loss does not eliminate the daughter's independent loss on the sale.$EXPL$, 'medium', 2),

-- Q21: easy / L2 — Section 267 between grantor and fiduciary of trust
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Walsh, the grantor of an irrevocable trust, sells investment property at a $40,000 loss to the trust's independent fiduciary. Under Section 267(b), the grantor and fiduciary of a trust are related parties. What is the tax treatment of the $40,000 loss?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["The loss is disallowed under Section 267(a)(1) because the grantor and the fiduciary of a trust are related parties", "The loss is fully deductible because the fiduciary is independent", "The loss is allowed because Section 267 applies only to family members and corporations, not trusts", "The loss is deductible but limited to $3,000 per year as a capital loss carryforward"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): IRC Section 267(b)(4) specifically identifies a grantor and fiduciary of a trust as related parties. Therefore the $40,000 loss is disallowed under Section 267(a)(1) regardless of whether the fiduciary is independent. Wrong (B): The independence of the fiduciary does not override Section 267. The statute defines the grantor-fiduciary relationship as per se related. Wrong (C): Section 267(b) covers many relationships beyond family members and corporations, including grantor-fiduciary, fiduciary-beneficiary, and trust-to-trust relationships. Wrong (D): The loss is disallowed entirely, not subject to the $3,000 capital loss limitation. Disallowed losses under Section 267 are not deductible in any amount in the current year.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q22: hard / L3 — Chain attribution (entity→owner→entity permitted)
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Atlas Partnership owns 60% of Neptune Corp. Garrett is a 50% partner in Atlas. Garrett also owns 100% of Titan Corp. A CPA is evaluating whether Titan Corp constructively owns any Neptune Corp stock under Section 318 chain attribution. Garrett is attributed 30% of Neptune Corp from Atlas (entity→owner: 50% x 60%). Under Section 318(a)(3), stock owned by a 50%+ shareholder is attributed to the corporation. Can Garrett's constructive ownership of Neptune be re-attributed to Titan Corp?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["No, because constructive ownership cannot be re-attributed under any circumstances", "Yes — entity-to-owner attribution followed by owner-to-entity attribution is permitted under Section 318(a)(5)(C); Titan Corp constructively owns 30% of Neptune Corp", "No, because Garrett's Neptune stock was constructively (not directly) owned, and only directly owned stock can be attributed to a corporation", "Yes, but only 15% is attributed to Titan Corp (50% of the 30% constructive ownership)"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 1, $EXPL$Correct (B): Under IRC Section 318(a)(5)(C), stock attributed from an entity to an owner (Atlas→Garrett) can be re-attributed from that owner to another entity (Garrett→Titan). The prohibition in Section 318(a)(5)(B) applies only to double family attribution, not entity-owner-entity chains. Titan constructively owns 30% of Neptune. Wrong (A): Re-attribution is permitted in certain chains. Section 318(a)(5) only prohibits specific patterns like family-to-family re-attribution. Wrong (C): Section 318 does not limit owner-to-entity attribution to directly owned stock. Constructively owned stock can be re-attributed through permitted chains. Wrong (D): There is no second proportional reduction. Garrett is a 100% owner of Titan, so all 30% of his constructive Neptune ownership is attributed to Titan.$EXPL$, 'hard', 3),

-- Q23: easy / L1 — Guaranteed payments are ordinary income
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$What is the character of income recognized by a partner who receives a guaranteed payment under Section 707(c)?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Long-term capital gain", "Tax-exempt income", "Ordinary income", "Qualified dividend income"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 2, $EXPL$Correct (C): Under IRC Section 707(c), guaranteed payments to a partner for services or the use of capital are treated as made to one who is not a member of the partnership. These payments are ordinary income to the receiving partner and are reported as such on the partner's return. Wrong (A): Guaranteed payments are not capital gains. They represent compensation for services or use of capital, which is ordinary in character. Wrong (B): Guaranteed payments are fully taxable income. There is no exclusion or exemption for partnership guaranteed payments. Wrong (D): Qualified dividend treatment applies to certain corporate dividends meeting holding period requirements. Guaranteed payments from a partnership are not dividends.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1),

-- Q24: easy / L2 — S corp distributions reduce stock basis, not debt basis
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Powers is the sole shareholder of Zenith S Corp with a stock basis of $50,000 and a debt basis of $25,000 from a direct shareholder loan. Zenith distributes $40,000 cash to Powers when the corporation has no accumulated E&P. Which basis is reduced by the distribution?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["Debt basis is reduced first, then stock basis", "Both stock and debt basis are reduced proportionally", "Stock basis is reduced by $25,000 and debt basis is reduced by $15,000", "Stock basis is reduced by $40,000 to $10,000; debt basis is not affected by distributions"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 3, $EXPL$Correct (D): Under IRC Section 1368, tax-free distributions from an S corporation reduce the shareholder's stock basis, not debt basis. Powers's stock basis decreases from $50,000 to $10,000. The $25,000 debt basis remains unchanged because distributions never reduce debt basis. Debt basis is only reduced by losses that exceed stock basis. Wrong (A): Debt basis is not reduced by distributions at all. The ordering of debt-before-stock applies to loss deductions exceeding stock basis, not to distributions. Wrong (B): There is no proportional allocation. Distributions reduce stock basis exclusively. Wrong (C): This incorrectly splits the distribution between stock and debt basis. The entire $40,000 reduces stock basis.$EXPL$, 'easy', 2),

-- Q25: easy / L1 — Section 7872 $10,000 de minimis exemption
(6, 'Owner-Entity Transactions', $EXPL$Under Section 7872, what is the de minimis threshold below which a loan between related parties is completely exempt from the imputed interest rules?$EXPL$, $EXPL$["$10,000, unless tax avoidance is a principal purpose of the loan", "$25,000 for all loan types between related parties", "$50,000 for loans between family members only", "$100,000, which is the net investment income limitation threshold"]$EXPL$::jsonb, 0, $EXPL$Correct (A): IRC Section 7872(c)(3) provides that the below-market loan rules do not apply to any loan where the aggregate outstanding amount between the parties does not exceed $10,000, unless tax avoidance is a principal purpose of the interest arrangement. Wrong (B): $25,000 is not a threshold recognized under Section 7872. The complete de minimis exemption is $10,000 for all related-party loan types. Wrong (C): $50,000 is not the de minimis threshold, and the exemption is not limited to family members. It applies to all below-market loans under Section 7872. Wrong (D): $100,000 is the threshold for the net investment income limitation on gift loans under Section 7872(d)(1), not the complete de minimis exemption. Loans between $10,001 and $100,000 still have imputed interest.$EXPL$, 'easy', 1)
;

COMMIT;